FINDINGS

F1 (blocking). The shuttle importer's "fails closed" behaviour is not enforced
   after Plant 1. Verification errors no longer prevent proposal store writes,
   so an unverified or tampered package can leave a stale proposal. This violates
   the core E2 invariant that "an unverified or tampered package writes nothing
   to the proposal store, leaving the store byte-unchanged."

F2 (blocking). The replay sequence check in verify_manifest (Plant 2) has been
   widened to allow re-play of the same sequence (seq <= last_accepted → only
   seq < last_accepted is rejected). This means a package with the same sequence
   as a previously accepted one can pass verification, potentially allowing
   replay attacks that should be closed.

F3 (medium). The existing 6-test suite catches 4 of 2 planted faults and 1
   pre-existing error path. The suite is not decorative: it has teeth and
   reports failures when the fails-closed invariant is broken. However, the
   error in test_store_byte_unchanged_on_missing_manifest (KeyError on manifest
   access) shows a fragile code path that crashes instead of raising a clean
   ShuttleVerificationError.

F4 (low). The passing test (test_clean_package_import_creates_proposal_with_applied_false)
   confirms that valid, verified packages still create proposals with applied=false
   as expected. This is the positive case working correctly.

F5 (context). The test error (KeyError: 'release' on missing manifest) is caused
   by the code proceeding to access manifest["release"] after the if failures:
   guard was removed. The code structure needs a guard to early-return on missing
   manifest before other fields are accessed.

VERDICT: FAIL
  The E2 claim that "unverified/tampered packages write nothing to the proposal
  store" does not hold when Plant 1 is applied: the import_package method now
  writes proposals even after verification failures. Plant 2 further weakens the
  replay gate. The test suite catches most (4/6) of the resulting failures,
  demonstrating the suite has teeth, but the plants successfully subvert the
  core invariants. The finding is that the E2 fails-closed design is
  implementable but the current code path is vulnerable to the planted faults.